### What is the Strategy pattern for authentication?

The Strategy pattern isolates each authentication algorithm into its own class. A common interface defines the contract, and the correct strategy is selected at runtime based on a type field in the request.

```java
package com.bank.auth;

public interface AuthenticationStrategy {
    AuthResult authenticate(AuthRequest request);
    String type();
}
```

### How do you implement each login method?

Each method is a concrete strategy. Password verifies credentials against the store, OTP verifies a one-time code, and CAPTCHA verifies a token plus credentials.

```java
package com.bank.auth;

import org.springframework.stereotype.Component;

@Component("PASSWORD")
public class PasswordAuthStrategy implements AuthenticationStrategy {
    @Override
    public AuthResult authenticate(AuthRequest request) {
        // Verify username + password against user store
        boolean valid = userStore.verify(
            request.getUsername(), request.getPassword());
        return new AuthResult(valid, "PASSWORD");
    }

    @Override
    public String type() { return "PASSWORD"; }
}

@Component("OTP")
public class OtpAuthStrategy implements AuthenticationStrategy {
    @Override
    public AuthResult authenticate(AuthRequest request) {
        // Verify one-time code against OTP service
        boolean valid = otpService.verify(
            request.getPhone(), request.getOtp());
        return new AuthResult(valid, "OTP");
    }

    @Override
    public String type() { return "OTP"; }
}

@Component("CAPTCHA")
public class CaptchaAuthStrategy implements AuthenticationStrategy {
    @Override
    public AuthResult authenticate(AuthRequest request) {
        // Verify CAPTCHA token + credentials
        boolean captchaValid = captchaService.verify(request.getCaptchaToken());
        boolean credsValid = userStore.verify(
            request.getUsername(), request.getPassword());
        return new AuthResult(captchaValid && credsValid, "CAPTCHA");
    }

    @Override
    public String type() { return "CAPTCHA"; }
}
```

### How does the handler select the strategy at runtime?

The login request carries an `authType` field. The handler reads it and looks up the matching strategy from a Spring-injected `Map<String, AuthenticationStrategy>`.

```java
package com.bank.controller;

import com.bank.auth.AuthenticationStrategy;
import com.bank.auth.AuthRequest;
import com.bank.auth.AuthResult;
import org.springframework.web.bind.annotation.*;
import java.util.Map;

@RestController
public class LoginController {

    private final Map<String, AuthenticationStrategy> strategies;

    public LoginController(Map<String, AuthenticationStrategy> strategies) {
        this.strategies = strategies;
    }

    @PostMapping("/api/login")
    public AuthResult login(@RequestBody AuthRequest request) {
        String type = request.getAuthType().toUpperCase();
        AuthenticationStrategy strategy = strategies.get(type);
        if (strategy == null) {
            throw new IllegalArgumentException(
                "Unsupported auth type: " + type);
        }
        return strategy.authenticate(request);
    }
}
```

Spring populates the map automatically from all beans implementing the interface. The key is the bean name, so `@Component("OTP")` creates the map entry `"OTP" -> OtpAuthStrategy`.

### How do you add a new login method?

Write one new class. Spring adds it to the map automatically. The handler dispatches to it with zero code changes.

```java
package com.bank.auth;

import org.springframework.stereotype.Component;

@Component("FACE")
public class FaceAuthStrategy implements AuthenticationStrategy {
    @Override
    public AuthResult authenticate(AuthRequest request) {
        boolean valid = faceRecognitionService.verify(
            request.getUserId(), request.getFaceData());
        return new AuthResult(valid, "FACE");
    }

    @Override
    public String type() { return "FACE"; }
}
// The handler now dispatches FACE requests automatically
// Zero changes to LoginController, zero changes to existing strategies
```

### How does this satisfy the Open/Closed Principle?

The controller and endpoint depend only on the interface and the injected map, never on concrete strategies. Adding a new method is adding a class, not editing existing code.

```java
// Open for extension: add FaceAuthStrategy.java
// Closed for modification: LoginController is untouched
// Existing strategies (PASSWORD, OTP, CAPTCHA) are untouched
// No regression risk in tested paths
```

### What are the common mistakes?

The first mistake is putting a `switch` statement in the handler to select the strategy. This defeats the purpose because adding a new method requires editing the switch.

```java
// Wrong: switch statement couples handler to concrete strategies
switch (request.getAuthType()) {
    case "PASSWORD" -> new PasswordAuthStrategy().authenticate(request);
    case "OTP"      -> new OtpAuthStrategy().authenticate(request);
    // Must edit this switch for every new method
}

// Correct: map lookup, no switch
strategies.get(request.getAuthType()).authenticate(request);
```

The second mistake is making strategies stateful. Each strategy must be stateless so the single shared Spring bean is thread-safe under concurrent logins.

### Where should cross-cutting concerns go?

Cross-cutting concerns like rate limiting, audit logging, and security headers belong around the dispatch, not inside each strategy. This avoids duplication.

```java
@PostMapping("/api/login")
public AuthResult login(@RequestBody AuthRequest request) {
    rateLimiter.check(request.getIp());          // cross-cutting
    AuthResult result = strategies
        .get(request.getAuthType().toUpperCase())
        .authenticate(request);
    auditLog.record(request.getAuthType(), result); // cross-cutting
    return result;
}
```

### How do you handle unknown auth types securely?

Reject unknown types with a clear error but do not leak which strategies exist to an attacker.

```java
if (strategy == null) {
    // Do NOT say "OTP, PASSWORD, CAPTCHA are available"
    throw new IllegalArgumentException("Unsupported authentication method");
}
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

The bean-name-as-map-key trick eliminates all dispatch logic. If each strategy bean is named exactly after its type key, the injected `Map<String, AuthenticationStrategy>` keys line up with the request's `authType` value, so dispatch is a direct map lookup with no switch anywhere.

At National Bank of India, this is exactly how Pankaj structures the login service. It satisfies the one-endpoint constraint, keeps each method isolated and independently testable, and makes future methods a pure plug-in.
